\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Marine inertial wave-motion estimation must reject low-frequency integration
drift without suppressing genuine oscillatory displacement. Accelerometer bias
and attitude error are particularly troublesome horizontally, where gravity
leakage competes with surge and sway. A regularizer appropriate for one sea
can distort another as the dominant period and acceleration level change.

Recent approaches include discrete Kalman reconstruction, adaptive IMU
filtering, nonlinear observers, and spectral methods
\cite{Reis2023_DiscreteKalmanHeave,Lu2023_AdaptiveDelayFreeHeave,
Li2025_HeaveCKF,YurovskyKudinov2025_IMUwaves,Hope2025_SFY}.
The Ornstein--Uhlenbeck process itself and Singer-type acceleration priors are classical
\cite{UhlenbeckOrnstein1930_OU,Singer1970_ManeuverModel}; the contribution here
is their integration with a fully specified three-dimensional quaternion MEKF,
integral-displacement regularization, and measurement-driven adaptation.

The 21-state estimator couples attitude and sensor biases to exact OU-driven
$[v,p,S,a_w]$ chains. A zero pseudo-measurement on $S$ suppresses slow drift.
A separate measurement-only front end estimates period and physical
band-limited acceleration, schedules the OU prior, and sets the integral
regularizer through a reduced physical drift--distortion objective
(SpectralMSE). Paired comparisons and a four-arm adaptation ablation isolate
the contribution of this regularizer from that of OU scheduling.

The displacement is oscillatory vessel motion about a local reference, not
geodetic position. Evaluation uses synthetic vessel-response records at one
fixed heading; it does not establish independent-vessel, sea-trial, thermal
calibration, or embedded resource performance. The OU prior is isotropic,
whereas the deployed integral regularizer uses distinct horizontal factors.
